        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 417,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 83,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Incompressible Navier-Stokes equations"
      },
      {
        "container_path": "_staging/total-read/model-audit/XBeach/connectivity/nonhydro-read/body-field-recovery/body-cached.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 8264,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 418,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 84,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Free surface flows in coastal areas are governed by the Navier Stokes equations. For the modelling of free surface gravity waves it suffices to restrict ourselves to: (i) Incompressible and (ii) Homogeneous (constant density, temperature, etc.) and (iii) Newtonian flow. In this case the Navier-stokes equations can be written in Cartesian coordinates as (Batchelor, 1967):"
      }
    ],
    "following_nonempty_paragraphs": [
      {
        "container_path": "_staging/total-read/model-audit/XBeach/connectivity/nonhydro-read/body-field-recovery/body-cached.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 8338,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 420,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 86,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Where u = [u(x,t),v(x,t),w(x,t)] is the velocity vector, P(x,t)  the pressure, g = [0,0,-g] the gravitational body force and  the deviatoric viscous stress tensor. Furthermore  denotes the tensor product of the vectors a and b."
      },
      {
        "container_path": "_staging/total-read/model-audit/XBeach/connectivity/nonhydro-read/body-field-recovery/body-cached.docx",
        "member": "word/document.xml",
        "start_xml_ordinal": 8571,
        "body_paragraph_zero_based": 422,
        "direct_body_paragraph_zero_based": 88,
        "paragraph_id": null,
        "text": "Usually the largest contribution to the pressure in a water column is due to the weight of the water above, or the hydrostatic pressure component. This can be made explicit in the equations by decomposing the pressure in a hydrostatic and hydrodynamic part or"
      }
    ],
